{"t":1790795915,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"4722d814-78e2-48dc-809d-641af957835d","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-30T18:19:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 301819\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n219 PM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nAll key messages were updated. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Temperatures will remain above normal through Friday.\n\n- 2) A pair of cold fronts will move across the area in the next\n     five days, one Friday evening, and a second one Monday.\n\n- 3) A strong area of Canadian high pressure brings frost and\n     freeze conditions beginning Monday night. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Temperatures will remain above normal through\nFriday.\n\nUpper level ridge will continue to build over the region,\ncresting over the area by the end of Thursday. High clouds\nassociated with a passing shortwave-trough to the north tonight \nshould prevent widespread fog development, but some river \nvalley fog is still possible west of I-81. Otherwise, fair \nweather will continue through Fri morning along with rising \ntemperatures. Temps should climb into the mid 80s Thursday under\nabundant sunshine and approach 90 on Friday under high clouds \nbefore showers arrive in the afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A pair of cold fronts will move across the area\nin the next five days, one Friday evening, and a second one\nMonday.\n\nThe first front will cross the area Fri evening. Expect\nscattered to numerous showers and perhaps a thunderstorm as the\nfront crosses the area. Not a lot of upper support with this\nfront and neither a lot of instability, so only light to \nmoderate rainfall amounts are expected. The front will stall \nacross the southern Mid-Atlantic states Saturday while high \npressure builds to the north. This will provide for a cooler, \ndry day Saturday. \n\nA wave of low pressure develops along the front in the southern\nAppalachians Sunday bringing the threat of showers again to the\narea Sunday into Sunday evening. \n\nA second stronger cold front associated with a deepening trof\nsouth of James Bay will cross the area Monday morning. This\nappears to be a dry frontal passage, but bring a significant\ncool down for the first full week of October.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A strong area of Canadian high pressure brings \nfrost and freeze conditions beginning Monday night. \n\nUpper level troughing pivots over the east coast Monday and Tuesday, \npushing a potent area of Canadian high pressure over the Mid-\nAtlantic. While overnight temperatures east of the Blue Ridge stay \nin the 40s overnight, lows along and west of the Blue Ridge will \ndrop into the upper 20s and 30s. Frost and freeze conditions are \npossible both Monday and Tuesday nights, mainly along the \nAlleghenies although could extend into the Shenandoah Valley. Low \ntemperatures in the Alleghenies will be in the upper 20s to low 30s \nwith the Shenandoah valley dipping into the mid 30s. \n\nWith high pressure overhead, dry conditions are expected both days \nwith weakening winds. High temperatures on Monday will be in the 60s \nfor most with those at higher elevations staying in the 50s. Tuesday \nwill be noticeably cooler during the day with highs in the 50s and \n60s. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\n\nFair weather tonight through Friday morning. Scattered showers\nand a perhaps a thunderstorm are expected Fri afternoon and\nevening with a cold front. Flight restrictions appear unlikely\nwith this frontal passage. \n\nA front stalled across the Carolinas brings increased precipitation \nchances across the terminals Saturday night and Sunday. Sub-VFR\nconditions are possible during times of precipitation with \nlower CIGs also possible due to the nearby front. Winds blow 5 \nto 10 knots out of the north/northeast on Saturday before \nshifting to southerly on Sunday. Winds remain light on Sunday. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nWinds will be on the increase through Fri night. SCA conditions\nare possible Fri night with the first frontal passage. \n\nWinds blow out of the north/northeast on Saturday before shifting to \nsoutherly on Sunday. Small Craft Advisory conditions are likely \nSaturday morning before winds decrease throughout the day. Winds are \nexpected to remain below SCA criteria on Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\n\nSome uncertainty continues to exist for the remainder of the \nweek as tide levels remain elevated and subtly fluctuating.\nThese small changes will make the difference in whether there is\nadditional minor flooding. Alexandria, Dahlgren, Annapolis, and\nHavre de Grace have the greatest chance of reaching flood stage. \nThe evening high tides are favored for any flooding since they \nare astronomically higher. A cold frontal passage should lead to\ndeclining water levels Friday night.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Coastal Flood Advisory from 6 PM this evening to midnight EDT \n     tonight for MDZ014.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...AVS/LFR\nAVIATION...AVS/LFR\nMARINE...AVS/LFR\n"},"forecast":{"t":1790795880,"days":{"2026-09-30":{"hi":81,"lo":63,"pop":0,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-01":{"hi":84,"lo":66,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-02":{"hi":88,"lo":61,"pop":40,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-03":{"hi":69,"lo":57,"pop":23,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers then Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-04":{"hi":67,"lo":58,"pop":39,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-10-05":{"hi":70,"lo":49,"pop":9,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-06":{"hi":61,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1790794800,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,24500,24700,null,23300,23800,24700,24100,24600,24500,24200,23600,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,nul